\subtree{
\title{5. What follows}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher needs no GraalVM.} With the same flags a stock OpenJDK 25 is as fast as the GraalVM JDK within the guard's 3 \% on every corpus (the largest loss 1.9 \%, the geometric mean 0.7 \%; six rounds a corpus over both flag sets: 1.6 \% and 0.4 \%), accepts and rejects the same, and computes the same.}
\li{\strong{What "the platform" of the launcher's number is}: HotSpot of OpenJDK 25 - the tiered JIT with C2 on top and eight compiler threads, the serial collector with a heap of 7 GB and a young generation of 1 GB, transparent huge pages - on eight performance cores of the development host. On the GraalVM JDK the launcher runs exactly that, with JVMCI enabled and idle (section 2). The warm pass of Mathlib in 11.0 s is a number of OpenJDK, not of GraalVM.}
\li{\strong{GraalVM's part in RLVM} is two things: the native image, which is the release and the one executable (GraalVM CE's \code{native-image}), and the Truffle tier of the machine, which is off by default and which on a stock JDK has only the fallback runtime (its probe fails there, section 2). Neither is touched by this result.}
\li{\strong{What the flags are worth} is a second result: with its own defaults the stock VM needs 8.5 \% more for a warm pass and 12.5 \% more for the cold pass than with the launcher's line (section 4.5; the defaults of the GraalVM JDK were not timed here). A JDK alone is not the platform; the JDK with that line is.}
\li{\strong{Nothing has to change}: the default java of the launcher is the build's JDK, and that is GraalVM CE because the build needs it for the image. What the result allows is to say so in [the platform note](design-platform-document) and in the tables that name the launcher's platform, and, if the maintainer wants the launcher to run on whatever JDK 25 a developer has, the changes of section 5.1.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 What would have to change for the launcher and the build to take any JDK 25}

\p{Described, not done: no tracked file was edited. Each item names what was seen to fail or to hold on the stock JDK.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The launcher's flag line} (\code{JVM_FLAGS} in \src{scripts/build.sh}, the template of \code{rlvm-jvm}): \code{\verb~|-XX:-UseJVMCICompiler~} stops a stock VM at its start (section 2). Written as \code{\verb~|-XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler~} the same line starts both VMs and still switches GraalVM's compiler off as the JIT where there is one; the other way is to leave the flag out when the JDK's \code{release} file has no \code{GRAALVM_VERSION}. The same flag is in the test VM of \src{rlvm-truffle/pom.xml} and in the constants of \src{scripts/platform-matrix.py} and \src{bench/modules/rlvm-export/measure-all.sh}.}
\li{\strong{The launcher's java}: the template writes the build's \code{\verb~|$JAVA_HOME/bin/java~} into the launcher and falls back to \code{java} on the path; \code{RLVM_JAVA} overrides both. That already takes any JDK. What it does not do is say which JDK it found: a JDK older than 25 fails on the class-file version with status 1, like the refused flag.}
\li{\strong{\src{env.sh}} sets \code{JAVA_HOME} to \code{\verb~|.toolchain/graalvm-ce-25~} for every script (\src{scripts/build.sh}, \src{scripts/dev.sh}, \src{scripts/steward-install.sh}). It is the one place where the JDK of Maven is chosen; it takes no value from outside. \src{scripts/check-light.sh}, \src{bench/docker/entrypoint.sh} and \src{bench/modules/rlvm-nat/natbench.py} name the same directory themselves.}
\li{\strong{The Maven step works as it is.} With the stock JDK as \code{JAVA_HOME} it compiles every shipped module and gives the same 22 jars byte for byte (section 3.4): the Truffle API, runtime and compiler are Maven artifacts, and no source of RLVM names a class of \code{\verb~|jdk.vm.ci~} or of the Graal compiler.}
\li{\strong{\src{scripts/build.sh} stops on a stock JDK in three places}, by reading: it reads \code{GRAALVM_VERSION} from \code{\verb~|$JAVA_HOME/release~} and dies when there is none (a stock JDK has none; the version of the Truffle artifacts is also the property \code{graalvm.version} of \src{pom.xml}, which the script already reads to compare); a build with the executable needs \code{\verb~|bin/native-image~}, which only GraalVM has; and its verification of the launcher ends with \code{\verb~|--truffle-probe~} and demands \code{\verb~|OK: runtime compilation works~}, which a stock JDK answers with status 3 (measured). For a \code{\verb~|--jvm-only~} distribution on a stock JDK the first needs the fallback to the pom and the third needs the probe to be a check of the Truffle tier and not of the launcher: skipped, or expected to say "fallback runtime", when the JDK is not a GraalVM. \code{BUILD-INFO.txt} has a line \code{graalvm:} that would have to name the JDK instead.}
\li{\strong{\src{scripts/dev.sh}} needs nothing of its own: \code{dist} and \code{dist-quick} are \code{\verb~|build.sh --jvm-only~}, \code{build} / \code{test} / \code{install} are Maven with \src{env.sh}, \code{info} prints the JDK it finds. \code{dist-native}, \code{dist-tree}, \code{release} and the Oracle commands build an image and stay with GraalVM.}
\li{\strong{The tests}: \code{rlvm-cli} passes on the stock JDK with its two compilation tests skipped; \code{rlvm-truffle} needs the flag of item 1 and, for what it tests, a JDK with the Graal compiler. The other modules' suites and the gates were not run on a stock JDK (section 6).}
\li{\strong{\src{scripts/fetch-toolchain.sh}} fetches GraalVM CE and nothing else. The executable needs it in any case; a stock JDK would be a second, optional download with its own pinned checksum.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. What was not measured}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A difference of about 1 \%} between the JDKs: three interleaved rounds per pair, six over both pairs, resolve about 3 \% on the passes under a second and 1 to 2 \% on the three large corpora. With the statistic that includes the passes in which the JIT still works, two corpora of one pair and one of the other are above 3 \% and do not repeat (section 4.2); more rounds on Init, cedar and reachability were not taken.}
\li{\strong{A profile of a pass} on either JDK (JFR): not taken, there being no difference above 3 \% that repeats.}
\li{\strong{x86-64}, the arena's architecture: everything here is the aarch64 development host.}
\li{\strong{Another stock build}: one vendor (Eclipse Temurin), one patch level (25.0.5.1+2, newer than the 25.0.5.0.3 the GraalVM JDK is built from). Not the OpenJDK archive of jdk.java.net, no other vendor, no JDK newer than 25.}
\li{\strong{The reference engine by name and the strict policy} on the stock JDK, as timings (the launcher's other heap line, 13 GB). The reference engine did run there: it confirms every rejection of the 109 rejected arena tests and decides the units the machine declines.}
\li{\strong{The gates} of \src{scripts/gate.sh} (230 published tests, 17,775 mutants, 1,196 hostile rows) and the test suites of the modules other than \code{rlvm-cli} and \code{rlvm-truffle} on the stock JDK. What stands in for them: the 270 arena tests, the corpora with 8, 4 and 1 workers, and the counters of the one-worker runs.}
\li{\strong{Fewer than 8 workers, the cold process from start to exit with its start-up, instruction counts}: the cold row is pass 1 of a process that goes on; no \code{perf} counters were taken.}
\li{\strong{The flags one by one} on the stock JDK: only the huge-page advice was taken out alone. The row with the VM's defaults changes the collector, the compiler threads and the young generation together.}
\li{\strong{The Truffle tier on a stock JDK with the Graal compiler added} on the upgrade module path: not tried; the tier is off by default.}
}
}
